# G017 — Pig Latin

*v2. See `pig-latin.md` for prose: transformation rule applied per-word
via `@map`, pattern matching on content structure (vowel vs consonant
start), reversible cipher, capitalization as presentation separate
from content.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Content Transformation Pipeline

[transform-document @document @ruleset
    IN ORDER TO
        apply a rule-based transformation per-word
        preserving presentation (capitalisation, trailing punctuation)
        while the content itself is rewritten

    @words <- [tokenise {text: @document}]
    @transformed <- @words mapped to
        [pig-latin-word {word: @w, ruleset: @ruleset}]
    @result <- @transformed joined with spaces
    -> @result
]

---

## Verb Surface

[pig-latin
    {text}                                   ;; -> transformed text
]

[pig-latin-word
    {word, ruleset}                           ;; -> transformed single word
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: pattern match `where` first letter is vowel or consonant

[pig-latin-word {word: @w, ruleset: @r}]
    IN ORDER TO
        apply one of two branches based on the word's opening sound
        while preserving capitalisation and trailing punctuation
        so the presentation layer is transferred, not recomputed

    @stripped <- strip trailing punctuation from @w
    @was-capitalised <- first letter of @stripped:text is uppercase
    @lowered <- @stripped:text lowercased

    @transformed <-
        @lowered
            where first letter is vowel
                -> @lowered + "yay"
            otherwise
                @cluster <- take-while consonant from @lowered
                @rest    <- drop @cluster-length from @lowered
                -> @rest + @cluster + "ay"

    @final <- @transformed
        where @was-capitalised -> capitalise first letter
        otherwise               -> as-is

    return @final + @stripped:trailing-punctuation
]

Content and presentation are separate concerns. The rule rewrites
content; the choreography coordinates both — preserving
capitalisation, capturing trailing punctuation, transferring each
to the new position.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [pig-latin {text: "Hello World"}]
    ;; -> "Ellohay Orldway"

    -> [pig-latin {text: "Eat apples!"}]
    ;; -> "Eatyay applesyay!"
    ;; (vowel-start: +yay; consonant-start: move cluster + ay)
]
